.TH HTTP 3
.SH NAME
http \- HTTP/1.x client with streaming requests and responses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <http.h>
.P
.B #include <http.h>
.br
.B typedef struct http_s http;
.SH DESCRIPTION
The http module is a client for HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1 over plain TCP or TLS.
It is made for the event loop of the io(3) module: nothing blocks, the
caller waits for the socket to become readable or writable and then calls
.BR http_canwrite (3)
or
.BR http_canread (3).
A blocking connection is possible by clearing
.I nonblocking
before the request is started.
.P
A request is described first and sent later, by the event loop. The steps are:
.TP
1.
.BR http_init (3)
prepares the
.B http
structure.
.TP
2.
.BR http_open (3),
.BR http_get (3)
or
.BR http_post (3)
parse the URL, resolve the host, create the socket and start to connect.
.TP
3.
.BR http_header (3)
adds headers. The body is added with
.BR http_body (3)
(known length) or with
.BR http_chunked (3),
.BR http_chunk (3)
and
.BR http_chunk_end (3)
(chunked transfer encoding, data that is produced while it is sent).
.TP
4.
The event loop calls
.BR http_canwrite (3)
when the socket is writable and
.BR http_canread (3)
when it is readable. The first one sends the request, the second one reads and parses
the status line and the headers of the response.
.TP
5.
The body of the response is taken out with
.BR http_recv (3),
which decodes a chunked body, a body with a length or a body that ends with the connection.
.TP
6.
.BR http_close (3)
closes the connection and
.BR http_free (3)
releases the memory.
.P
For streamed answers, such as the server-sent events or the newline delimited JSON of
language model APIs, set
.I stream
in the structure before the request is sent and feed what
.BR http_recv (3)
returns to
.BR sse_feed (3)
or
.BR ndjson_feed (3).
.SS The structure
.nf
typedef struct http_s {
  fd_type sock;           socket, -1 when there is none
  struct { buffer out, in; } q;   output and input buffers
  stralloc host;          host name of the URL
  ipv4addr addr;          address of the host, 0.0.0.0 until resolved
  ipv4port port;          port, 80 or 443 by default
  http_request*  request; newest request, older ones by ->next
  http_response* response;newest response, older ones by ->next
  tls_t* ssl;
  int err;                errno (or TLS error) of the last operation
  unsigned nonblocking:1; default 1
  unsigned keepalive:1;   default 0, sends Connection: close
  unsigned connected:1;
  unsigned tls:1;         set by an https URL
  unsigned sent:1;        the request headers are out
  unsigned stream:1;      leave the body for http_recv
  uint16 version;         11 for HTTP/1.1, otherwise 1.0 for a GET
} http;
.fi
.SS Requests
.nf
typedef enum { GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH, HEAD } http_req_t;

typedef struct http_request_s {
  uint32 serial;
  http_req_t type;
  stralloc location;   path and query, as sent
  stralloc headers;    extra header lines, each ends in CRLF
  stralloc body;       queued body (chunked: already encoded)
  size_t body_pos;     how much of it is written
  unsigned chunked:1, body_done:1, queued:1;
  struct http_request_s* next;
} http_request;
.fi
.SS Responses
.nf
typedef struct http_response_s {
  http_transfer_type transfer;  how the body is framed
  http_status status;           where the response is
  int code;                     HTTP status code, -1 until the status line
  uint64 headers_len, data_pos, content_length, chunk_length;
  int chunk_state;              position of http_recv in a chunked body
  stralloc data, boundary;      status line and headers, multipart boundary
  int err;
  int (*header)(struct http_s*, const char*, size_t);
  struct http_response_s* next;
} http_response;
.fi
.P
.I transfer
is HTTP_TRANSFER_UNDEF (the body ends with the connection), HTTP_TRANSFER_CHUNKED,
HTTP_TRANSFER_LENGTH or HTTP_TRANSFER_BOUNDARY (multipart).
.I status
is HTTP_RECV_HEADER while the headers are read, HTTP_RECV_DATA after them,
HTTP_STATUS_CLOSED when the peer closed the connection, HTTP_STATUS_ERROR,
HTTP_STATUS_BUSY and HTTP_STATUS_FINISH when the body is complete.
If
.I header
is set it is called with every header line of the response, without its line end.
.SH EXAMPLE
A streamed POST with a chunked body, the answer written to standard output:
.P
.nf
http h;
char buf[4096];
ssize_t n;
fd_type fd;

http_init(&h, 0, 0);
h.version = 11;
h.stream = 1;
http_post(&h, "http://example.com/v1/chat");
http_header(&h, "Content-Type", "application/json");
http_chunked(&h);
http_chunk(&h, "{\e"prompt\e":\e"hi\e"}", 15);
http_chunk_end(&h);

while(h.response->status != HTTP_STATUS_FINISH) {
  io_wait();
  while((fd = io_canwrite()) != -1)
    if(fd == h.sock)
      http_canwrite(&h, io_onlywantread, io_onlywantwrite);
  while((fd = io_canread()) != -1)
    if(fd == h.sock) {
      http_canread(&h, io_onlywantread, io_onlywantwrite);
      while((n = http_recv(&h, buf, sizeof(buf))) > 0)
        write(1, buf, n);
      if(n < 0)
        break;
    }
}
http_close(&h);
http_free(&h);
.fi
.SH NOTES
Request headers and bodies are not escaped; names and values with a CR or LF
are refused by
.BR http_header (3).
The TLS client does not verify the certificate of the server.
.P
The functions http_read(3), http_read_header(3), http_read_internal(3) and
http_response_read(3) are the first reader of the module, written for
.BR buffer (3)
operations. They work with
.I stream
cleared. New code should use http_recv(3).
.SH SEE ALSO
http_init(3), http_open(3), http_header(3), http_chunk(3), http_canwrite(3),
http_canread(3), http_recv(3), http_close(3), http_free(3), sse_feed(3),
ndjson_feed(3), tls_servername(3), buffer_chunked(3)
